{"name":"Cumin Ochre","hex":"#a06600","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cumin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The flowering plant Cuminum cyminum, in the family Apiaceae.","Its aromatic long seed, used as a spice, notably in Indian, Middle Eastern, and Mexican cookery."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cumin"},{"term":"ochre","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clay earth pigment containing silica, aluminum and ferric oxide.","A somewhat dark yellowish orange colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a yellow-orange colour.","Referring to cultures that covered their dead with ochre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ochre"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cumin","blocks":[{"text":"Cumin is a flowering plant in the family Apiaceae, native to the Irano-Turanian Region. Its seeds – each one contained within a fruit, which is dried – are used in the cuisines of many cultures in both whole and ground form. Although cumin is used in traditional medicine, there is no high-quality evidence that it is safe or effective as a therapeutic agent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Cuminum_cyminum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-198.jpg/330px-Cuminum_cyminum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-198.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumin","wikidata":"Q132624"},{"title":"Ochre","blocks":[{"text":"Ochre is a family of natural clay earth pigments, made up of ferric oxide and varying amounts of clay and sand, ranging in colour from yellow to deep orange or brown. The term is also used for the colours produced by this pigment, especially a light brownish-yellow. A variant of ochre containing a large amount of hematite, or dehydrated iron oxide, has a reddish tint known as red ochre. The term \"ochre\" is colloquially used to cover a range of different substances used for their colours in Aboriginal Australian art and ceremonial decoration, for instance the clay known as \"white ochre\" or \"pipe clay\" is derived from kaolin or gypsum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG/330px-Hellocker-_Pigment.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ochre","wikidata":"Q12353401"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Sa_cumin.jpg/500px-Sa_cumin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Sa_cumin.jpg/1280px-Sa_cumin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Sa_cumin.jpg/500px-Sa_cumin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cumin seeds, about 5 mm long.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASa_cumin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Black_Cumin.jpg/500px-Black_Cumin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Black_Cumin.jpg/1280px-Black_Cumin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Black_Cumin.jpg/500px-Black_Cumin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black cumin seeds (Elwendia persica)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Cumin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Caraway_Seed.jpg/500px-Caraway_Seed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Caraway_Seed.jpg/500px-Caraway_Seed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser